      "claim": "In rats, VU6043653 showed a brain:plasma Kp of 1.68 at 0.25 h and was reported as having excellent CNS penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C05"],
      "claim": "In mice, LDN/OSU-0212320 maintained an average brain/plasma ratio of approximately 2 over 8 hours after intraperitoneal dosing, indicating BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "In a patient, imatinib CSF levels were almost 100-fold lower than plasma levels, indicating poor CNS penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Multiple high-scoring direct in-vivo rodent and human records demonstrate meaningful CNS access, including brain/plasma ratios, brain concentrations, and CSF detection, outweighing one contradictory imatinib poor-penetration record.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "One high-scoring evidence molecule (imatinib) provides direct contradictory poor-penetration evidence, introducing some uncertainty despite the overall positive balance.",
    "Some positive records rely on total brain/plasma ratios or CSF detection without unbound brain exposure data, but direct systemic CNS access is still demonstrated."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

